Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Offsets: where the offsets of a convex crease's faces become tangent
inside the solid, the intersection is the branch along the crease and its
continuation past the tangency, as one edge; a vertex that takes another's
place covers the other's points (mitcad#1193).

The case: a loft from a square to a circle inside it or through its
corners (BRepOffsetAPI_ThruSections), whose walls lean in towards the
circle, shelled inwards with both its ends removed, or with one, or offset
inwards closed. At the corners the loft's ruled faces meet at convex
creases that flatten to tangency at the circle. Patch 1130 covers walls
leaning out, where the inward offsets of a corner's two faces become
tangent past the circle's end. Leaning in, they become tangent inside the
solid (0.1 mm below the circle's end for a 1 mm wall, on it where the
circle runs through the corners), and the walls extended to the removed
end cross past the tangency along the branch's continuation and two
branches turning sideways, all starting tangent there. The pave filler
gives them as pieces joined by pieces of a few microns, not always
connected, sometimes the branch along the crease and a sideways one as one
curve. Patch 1130 left them all (the branch ends before the end plane),
and MakeLoops could not rebuild the inner walls from them: the thick solid
was not done (BRepOffset_UnknownError) or came out invalid, the closed
offset invalid or empty, for most thicknesses from 0.5 to 3 mm. Keeping
only the branch and its continuation as those pieces does not help:
AssembleEdge glues them into an edge of a tolerance of 50 mm, which Inter3D
drops, and the corners have no intersection edge at all.

DropCrossingsPastTangentEnd now finds where the offsets become tangent (the
tangent end's nearest point on the offset face) and, where that lies inside
the solid or within 1 % of the offset past its end, makes one edge
(theMerged): the branch along the crease (the edge 1130 picks) up to 1 %
of the offset before the tangency, the piece of the intersection, of all
its pieces, from within 5 % of the offset of the tangency that goes on
most nearly in the branch's direction from 1 % past it (and on into the
pieces that go on from its far end in that direction, where it is short),
and a curve tangent to both between them, each piece parametrised by its
length.
Inter3D gives that edge, with its curves on both offset faces, as the
intersection. Past the tangency the walls, extended to a removed end, thus
meet along the branch's continuation; the sideways crossings, slivers of
hundredths of a millimetre in the extension up to the end, are left out.
Only where the crease's faces are tangent at one end of the edge and not
at its middle and the intersection gave more than one piece; a single edge
that runs on along the branch itself is left as it is. Where the tangency
lies further past the end and the main branch reaches the end plane,
1130 is unchanged.

BRepOffset_MakeLoops substitutes vertices (myVerVerMap) and gives the new
one the old one's points on the edges' curves, which may lie further from
it than either's tolerance: the closed offset's and the one-end shell's
vertex where a corner's edge meets the end's offset was off its own
section edge by 6.4e-5 against a tolerance of 5.7e-5 (BRepCheck
InvalidPointOnCurve), and so in the closed offset of a square to a circle
of radius 20 (walls leaning out) at 2 mm. The new vertex's tolerance now
covers the points it takes over, with a margin of 0.1 % (CoverPoints); a
substitution whose points lay within the tolerance keeps it, but for the
margin.

The shells are valid, both ends open, with the volumes the one-end shells
and the closed offset give (within 0.05 mm3), the inner walls the
thickness from the outer ones within 1e-6 mm. OCCT's default (not
adaptive) volume integration of these bodies is off by up to 0.3 % (it is
exact with an accuracy given, and on a mesh).

Where no piece goes on from within 5 % of the offset of the tangency, a
piece from within 20 % is taken (mitcad#1217): with patch 1200, the walk
of the pave filler about the tangency at the corner of a half of the side
the loft splits (a square to a circle of radius 10, its seam on a side,
shelled 1 mm through the geometry facade) stopped 8 % of the offset short
of it on Windows, and the inner wall came out with a wire the wrong way
round; on Linux it passed.

An outward loft can need the same continuation when the pave filler stops
its main branch before the removed end: the octagon to radius 21.7 with
its circle seam at 22.5 degrees, shelled 0.5 mm. On macOS the branch
stopped 0.08229064 mm before the end plane while the tangency lay
0.01750246 mm past it. Leaving all crossings then produced no inner
walls, and MakeThickSolid reported UnknownError. Join the continuation
also when the main branch does not reach the end; its near piece may use
the same 20 % neighbourhood as the continuation. The existing validity,
wall-distance, rim, volume and closed-offset checks remain unchanged.

